Subject: Fold The Prologue Store-Load Pair And Minified `!0`

What this does
  Two rewrites in resolve_labels, both strict shrinks of the bytecode stream.
  A function whose body opens by reading a prologue-initialised binding starts
  with `put_loc(n) get_loc(n)`; that becomes `set_loc(n)`. And the sequence a
  minifier emits for a boolean constant -- `push_i32(0) lnot` for `!0`,
  `push_i32(v) lnot` for `!v` -- becomes `push_true` or `push_false`.

Why we need it
  Upstream already folds `put_x(n) get_x(n)` into `set_x(n)`, but the prologue
  initialisers are written straight into the output buffer before the rewriting
  loop starts, so their stores never pass through that case and the redundant
  load survives on every call, forever. The store is whichever initialiser was
  emitted last -- `this`, `new.target`, `arguments`, the home object, the active
  function, the function's self-reference or either variable-environment object
  -- which is the complete set, because the body's first instruction can only
  read one binding. The `lnot` fold exists because terser writes `true` as `!0`
  and `false` as `!1`: a production React Native bundle in this repository
  contains 478-488 of them, against seven foldable binary constants, so the
  bundler reintroduces exactly the pattern the engine can remove.

What it adds
  The store's stack effect is unchanged -- `put_loc` pops, `set_loc` does not,
  and the `get_loc` that followed pushed the value back -- so compute_stack_size
  and the interpreter are untouched. The prologue fold only fires when the store
  is still the last thing emitted, when the body's first instruction is not a
  branch target, and when it is not one of the shapes the element-load and
  borrow fusions take over: consuming that `get_loc` here would defeat
  `get_loc_field_nr` and `get_loc8_array_el` and reintroduce the dup/free round
  trip on the receiver. No opcode slot is spent and nothing is renumbered.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

